# ── Media ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The marketing videos and screenshots are what makes this repository ~890 MB on
# the server against a 195 MB checkout: every revision of a 3 MB .webm is still
# in the history. `binary` stops git treating them as text — no newline
# conversion on a Windows checkout, no attempt to produce a diff or a three-way
# merge of two encodes.
#
# Not Git LFS, deliberately. Moving them there rewrites every commit that ever
# touched one, which changes every SHA after the first video landed: every open
# pull request has to be rebased, every existing clone is detached from the new
# history, and every reference to a commit — in the changelog, in an audit note,
# in an issue — stops resolving. It also moves the cost rather than removing it,
# onto LFS bandwidth quota that a fork does not inherit. CONTRIBUTING documents
# `git clone --filter=blob:none` instead, which gives a contributor the same
# saving with no history change at all.
*.webm binary
*.mp4  binary
*.mov  binary
*.gif  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.avif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
